Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.990 align:start position:0%
 
this is tomorrow reading for reviewer  heidi kemps as the first major original  jrpg on new consoles and the latest  installment of a very long running  series tales of arias comes with a lot  of expectations attached ari sets out to  refresh his visual presentation and  gameplay to appeal to a new audience by  also tries his best to retain what has  made the tale series so beloved among  long-time fans fun characters fast-paced  combat and an epic sense of scale while  it managed to succeed admirably at most  of what it tries to do a few  shortcomings keep it from being the new  standard bearer for rpgs to come  [Music]  300 years ago the planet of dhana was  invaded by the people of their  neighboring star reyna and crumbled  beneath the might of the reinan's  advanced technology and knowledge since  their conquest the reinans have  destroyed the once vibrant dharnan  culture and enslaved the planet's people  one day a nameless amnesiac slave known  only as iron mask finds himself caught  up in a supply train hijacking by rebel  forces and discovers that the freight is  a random woman with a strange curse as  he gets swept up in the dharnan  rebellion iron mask discovers new powers  his true name alfin and a connection to  the ren and girl xion but this tiny  slave rebellion grows into something  much bigger the beginning of tales of  arrias is a marked departure from the  chipper banter and adventuring most  hell's games lead off with with heavy  topics like slavery and oppression  taking center stage in the narrative the  overall tone of arizo's story for the  first several hours is quite downward  much as i hate to admit it there aren't  many of us who can take on those ren and  armored soldiers drilling into you the  sheer misery and desperation of the don  and people fortunately once your party  fills out the familiar tales party  dynamics come back in full force with  characters personalities bouncing off  each other in numerous entertaining  dialogue exchanges it's okay i won't  hurt you  [Music]  crap the rapport among your teammates  and watching their interactions change  as they go through individual character  arcs is a major draw and you'll find  yourself eager to keep playing just to  see the team react to the latest turn of  events around the campfire or complain  about the latest broken dungeon elevator  it's good that the characters are so  likable because they really help carry  the story through some rough patches the  abuse and liberation of oppressed  peoples is a very challenging and  prescient subject matter to tackle and  generally tales of arise handles the  material well but at times it  disappoints by not diving beyond the  superficial level into some of the  difficult moral issues the story  presents the pacing can also feel rushed  as the plot frequently introduces  characters who we don't get to know but  are suddenly rendered very important to  current events and then quickly exit the  narrative after contributing their main  story be while the narrative and arises  later half shifts thematically many of  the same issues remain throughout like  most jrpgs the narrative entails of a  rise is very linear that isn't a bad  thing however as the constant promise of  new lands to explore or new dungeons to  delve into is a strong impetus to keep  moving you're also presented with  numerous optional side quests to tackle  should you so choose to and a very  convenient fast travel system makes it  easy to return to previous areas should  you want to do additional exploration or  resource gathering for crafting and  cooking there's also a surprisingly  satisfying fishing mini game at ponzura  arise that if you're not careful will  consume far too much of your playtime  but as beautiful as arizer's  environments are the big gameplay draw  lies in battles the tail series has  always leaned heavily on the appeal of  its robust action-oriented combat and  arises no different in this respect  when you encounter enemies during  exploration you'll be spirited away off  to a combat screen where you go toe to  toe with foes in a small arena during  combat you can freely move and execute a  variety of normal and special attacks  called arts which you assign to  controller buttons naturally you can  also jump and guard  dodge enemy attacks heavy emphasis is  placed on positioning dodging and  chaining attacks together to land  massive combos breaking down foe's  resistance to set up team-based finishes  called a boost strike while you can only  directly control one character at a time  you can give your three companions  detailed strategies to follow and their  ai tends to function quite well there  are some interesting new twists to  combat this time around however  offensive and restorative arts no longer  use the same resource pool as healing  spells and environmental interactions  now utilize a separate party-wide cure  point stock this separation allows for  all characters to focus more heavily on  offense during battle and there's no  need to worry about saving a specific  character's arts for healing in  emergencies though some characters like  shion will still need to be ready to  switch between fighting and healing when  needed and the cure points paul always  needs to be carefully monitored each  character is also given unique perks  that differentiate themselves and  establish their particular roles in  battle making each of them play wildly  differently from each other alfin can  sacrifice hp after using art to deal  extra damage sprightly mage rinwell can  charge and hold her magical arts to  chain together combos more easily  elegant knight kisara guards with her  massive shield rather than dodging and  performs enhanced arts out of her  guarding stance and so on each character  also has a unique limited use boost  attack that serves a distinct purpose  like law's armor shattering punches or  dohalim's movement restricting vines  many of these layers to combat aren't  revealed or aren't obvious right away  instead being rolled out over the course  of the game a good chunk of each  character's skills will need to be  opened up as you play through the skill  panel system where you spend sp earned  throughout their game to enhance your  character's abilities in combat there's  always something fresh and new being  added to the combat as a result making  tales of orisa's battles feel  consistently fresh when tales of arises  combat is at its best it feels like a  well-oiled machine with arts flying  enemies being smacked around and juggled  through the air armor being crushed and  guards being broken all topped off by a  spectacular finishing boot strike or  mystic art to crush foes into oblivion  but it's not always that smooth and  satisfying sometimes there's just so  much happening on screen at once with  much of it out of your control that it's  difficult to keep track of what arts  your squad is tossing at the enemy and  what attacks they're flinging back at  you particularly during boss fights or  when you're taking on a large enemy pack  it's not uncommon to find yourself  severely damaged or ko'd without fully  understanding why and while it's fairly  easy to pick yourself back up it's still  frustrating there were numerous times  when i felt like i was playing at my  best and still getting pummeled for  reasons that were unclear unlike many  other tales games arise doesn't offer  any co-op play so you can't rely on a  buddy to help keep things under control  either and then there's the dlc problem  while tales of auris offers some paid  cosmetic dlc extra costumes and  decorations which don't change gameplay  it also offers gameplay boosting  packages that grant bonuses like  permanent exp and sp boosts massive shop  discounts reducing all crafting and  cooking materials needed to one and so  on while this is an optional purchase  the fact that exists at all constantly  made me second guess the game's design  decisions are healing items expensive to  teach me to take less damage or to get  me to buy a shop discount dlc package  are boss fights so much harder than  standard fights because the designers  really want to test my skills or because  i didn't get that exp boost dlc i often  felt like money and resources like sp  were being kept scarce so instead of  focusing completely on having fun and  exploring tales of verizon's various  gameplay systems i was often wondering  if i was being subtly pushed to buy  gameplay advantages  taken as a whole tales of arias is a  very good rpg boasting beautiful visuals  a wonderful cast of characters and  engaging combat mechanics but his flaws  and their odious dlc are also difficult  to ignore  if you're looking for a lengthy charming  and engaging jrpg to play on your shiny  new console or pc gaming rig tails of a  rise is certainly a fine choice just  don't go into expecting an all-time  classic  [Music]  unsullied fertile land as far as the eye  can see  that's beautiful  [Applause]  what  no nothing just  i was thinking the very same thing

